module std.prelude
//! `Show` — rendering a value as text from code that does not know the value's type.

// **The gap this closes.** `print` and `"${…}"` pick a formatter per *concrete* type, so a
// value whose type is a **type parameter** could not be rendered at all: a `t` has no
// representation, and there is nothing to pick. The prelude's own `expect` hit this the
// day it was written — the natural draft reports what it got, `panic("expected ${value}")`,
// and none of it was expressible.
//
// **The trait is recognized by its method, not by its name.** The rewrite looks for an
// in-scope bound whose trait declares `show`, so a program may define its own and the
// compiler is none the wiser — the same rule arithmetic operator overloading follows. The
// name `Show` is what the *message* suggests, because this is the one the prelude ships.

/// Types that can be rendered as text.
///
/// A `where t: Show` bound is what lets a generic function format its own payload:
/// inside such a function `"${v}"` is rewritten to `v.show()`, so interpolation and
/// `println` work on a bare type parameter. Without a bound the diagnostic says so and
/// names this trait.
///
/// A concrete type with a `show` impl prints the same way — `println(pt)` and `"${pt}"`
/// both reach it.
///
/// # Examples
///
/// ```
/// let dump<t> where t: Show = (v: t) -> string => "got ${v}"
/// ```
pub trait Show {
  /// `self` rendered as text.
  ///
  /// It may not interpolate `self` in an impl *for* the type it is implemented on —
  /// `impl Show for Pt { show = (self) => "${self}" }` is refused rather than compiled
  /// into infinite recursion. Interpolate the fields instead.
  show: (Self) -> string
}

// The impls below are **ordinary Lyra**: `"${self}"` on a concrete primitive is exactly
// the formatter `print` already picks, so nothing here is a builtin. That is the same
// division of labour `parse_i64` and `trim` follow — anything expressible in the language
// belongs in the prelude, where it is readable, testable and replaceable, and the builtin
// registry keeps only what is genuinely primitive.

/// A string shows as itself.
///
/// It returns the value rather than interpolating it: `"${s}"` would copy the bytes into
/// a fresh box for no reason, and `show` is the one place that cost would be paid on
/// every rendering of every string.
impl Show for string { show = pure (self) => self }

/// Shows as `true` or `false`.
impl Show for bool { show = pure (self) => "${self}" }
/// Shows as the character itself, not as its code point.
impl Show for rune { show = pure (self) => "${self}" }

/// Shows in base 10, with a leading `-` when negative.
impl Show for i8 { show = pure (self) => "${self}" }
/// Shows in base 10, with a leading `-` when negative.
impl Show for i16 { show = pure (self) => "${self}" }
/// Shows in base 10, with a leading `-` when negative.
impl Show for i32 { show = pure (self) => "${self}" }
/// Shows in base 10, with a leading `-` when negative.
impl Show for i64 { show = pure (self) => "${self}" }
/// Shows in base 10, with a leading `-` when negative.
impl Show for i128 { show = pure (self) => "${self}" }

/// Shows in base 10.
impl Show for u8 { show = pure (self) => "${self}" }
/// Shows in base 10.
impl Show for u16 { show = pure (self) => "${self}" }
/// Shows in base 10.
impl Show for u32 { show = pure (self) => "${self}" }
/// Shows in base 10.
impl Show for u64 { show = pure (self) => "${self}" }
/// Shows in base 10.
impl Show for u128 { show = pure (self) => "${self}" }

/// Shows in decimal notation.
impl Show for f16 { show = pure (self) => "${self}" }
/// Shows in decimal notation.
impl Show for f32 { show = pure (self) => "${self}" }
/// Shows in decimal notation.
impl Show for f64 { show = pure (self) => "${self}" }
